If you searched "meta ai agent" this week, you probably landed on Muse, the personal agent Meta launched on September 8, 2026. It is one of at least ten products now competing for the same job: an assistant that has its own computer, keeps working after you close the app, and comes back to you when something needs a decision. OpenAI calls its version a dot, Anthropic has Duet and Cowork, xAI has Grok Bot, Google rebranded Spark as Tasks, and a long tail of independents (Manus, Genspark, Kimi, Perplexity) sells the same idea with different trade-offs.

This guide is not a copy of the head-to-head posts you will find elsewhere. It is organised around one question: which personal agent for which job? Every price, limit and availability claim below is dated "as of November 25, 2026" and comes from the vendor's own page where one loaded. Where two official pages disagree, we say so. We did not run hands-on tests on any of these products, and we explain exactly what we did and did not do in the evaluation section near the end.

{% youtube id="lKc_TigOiLA" title="I Tried Every AI Agent So You Don't Have To: ChatGPT Dots vs Muse vs Grok vs Claude vs Hark" /%}

*A third-party 2026 video comparing the main always-on agents side by side. It is a reviewer's opinion, not a vendor demo, and we cite it only for context.*

## Short answer: the best personal AI agent for each job

There is no single winner, because these products split along three lines: where the agent runs (its own cloud computer or your laptop), what it costs to get started, and how far it will act without asking you first. These are our picks as of November 25, 2026, based on the documented behaviour of each product.

| If you want... | Pick | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The cheapest way to try a real agent | Gemini Agent (Tasks in Gemini) | Free in six countries, with a spend cap and approval before purchases (calling and payments are US only) |
| A consumer agent that lives in your messaging apps | Meta Muse | Free tier, WhatsApp support, own secure VM, phone calls on paid tiers |
| The deepest "own a project for me" agent | ChatGPT Dots | Own cloud computer, 4,000+ app plugins, Slack and Teams, but Pro-priced today |
| A work-first inbox and calendar agent | Claude Duet | Proactive triage of what needs attention, approvals before sends, but US waitlist and research preview |
| A desktop agent for files, decks and spreadsheets | Claude Cowork | Cloud tasks since October 6, 2026, included in every paid Claude plan |
| A local agent that works on your own files | Kimi Work | Runs on your machine, up to 300 sub-agents, free download |
| A general agent with a free daily credit pool | Manus | Free plan with daily credits, strong at research and slides |
| One workspace for slides, sheets and research | Genspark | Super Agent with its own project computer, free plan available |
| A bot team that works inside your SaaS tools | Grok Bot | Shared persistent cloud computer, works where there is no API |
| An agent inside your browser | Perplexity Comet | Free browser, but the acting agent needs a paid plan |

Three honest caveats before the detail. First, "personal agent" launches are recent (Kimi Work in June 2026, Grok Bot in August, Muse and Dots in September, Duet in October), so features and prices are moving weekly. Second, several of these are limited to the United States or exclude the EU, UK and Switzerland. Third, none of the vendors publishes an independent benchmark for personal-agent quality, so we do not rank on a score. The order of the sections below follows search demand (Muse, Dots and Cowork first), not a quality ranking. The table above is the verdict.

