## How we define a "personal agent" in this guide

We kept the definition narrow so the comparison stays fair. A product qualifies if a normal person can hand it a goal and walk away, it can act in real accounts (mail, calendar, shopping, files) rather than only draft text, and it asks before consequential steps. That excludes plain chatbots, and it also excludes developer-only coding agents. It includes browser agents and desktop agents as long as they take multi-step actions for you.

Two product names need a note. "Gemini Agent" was announced in November 2025 as an experimental feature for Google AI Ultra subscribers in the US. Google's own agent line later moved through the name Gemini Spark (a 24/7 agent for AI Ultra in May 2026) and, on November 17, 2026, was rebranded as Tasks in Gemini and made free. We list it as Gemini Agent because that is the name many people still search, and we describe the current Tasks product. "Claude Cowork" and "Claude Duet" are two different Anthropic products, covered separately below.

## The 10 personal AI agents, one by one

### Meta Muse

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/meta-muse.png" name="Meta Muse" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Muse is Meta's personal agent. Meta's own site describes it as an agent that "gets it done for you": it sorts email, books reservations, shops, manages calendars and builds fitness plans. The launch thread by Alexandr Wang on September 8, 2026 called it "always-on, wicked fast", able to use a browser and connect to your apps, and "designed to be secure".

{% x id="2097402344061510004" /%}

**How it works.** Each user gets a persistent, isolated Linux computer with a full browser, which Meta calls Muse Secure VM. You and the agent can both use it, and you can step in at any point. Meta's safety post (security.muse.ai) adds that the agent runs in an isolated cell, does not see real credentials, and sends every interaction with the outside world through a Sentinel that the agent cannot override. Meta says Muse checks with you before important actions such as sending an email or making a purchase. You chat with it in the Muse app on mobile or desktop, or directly in WhatsApp, by text or voice, according to muse.ai.

{% figure src="https://about.fb.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Introducing-Muse_-Personal-AI-Agent_SocialShare.jpg?w=1200" alt="Meta's Introducing Muse announcement graphic" caption="Meta's launch graphic for Muse. Source: Meta Newsroom, about.fb.com" /%}

**Who it is for.** People who want a general life-admin agent and already live in Meta's apps. Muse leans consumer: goals like saving money, shopping, fitness and relationships appear on the product page. Meta also lists a separate Muse for Business offering for small businesses, which we did not evaluate.

**Key features.** Secure VM with browser, connectors (email, calendar and Instagram are named on the product page), an audit trail of what the agent has done and plans to do, approval before actions like sending email or purchases, WhatsApp and voice, and proactive goal suggestions. Paid plans add phone calls.

**Pros**

- A free tier that includes a persistent VM and a mobile app, subject to a usage limit (Gemini Tasks is the other free option here).
- Documented safety design: sentinel check, credential isolation, audit trail.
- WhatsApp access lowers the friction to try it.

**Cons**

- Meta says Muse and its subscriptions are "in limited testing" and "aren't available in all locations yet". The launch was US first.
- The free usage limit is not published in numbers on the help page we read.
- Meta itself says Muse can and will still make mistakes, and the safety post describes the design as limiting damage rather than preventing errors.

**Benefits for you.** If you want an agent that texts you on an app you already use and does boring errands, Muse has the lowest barrier of the bigger vendors. The free tier means you can test the proactive behaviour before paying.

**Pricing (Meta Help Center, as of November 25, 2026).** Free with a usage limit. Power: $20 per month, 500M Muse tokens per week and 10 phone calls per day. Maximum: $100 per month, 3B tokens per week and 20 phone calls per day. Plans renew monthly.

**Free tier and limits.** Free with an unpublished weekly allowance. You either upgrade or wait for the limit to refresh. Phone calls are a paid-plan allowance.

**Standout use case.** Handing over a recurring errand (reservations, price checks, calendar clean-up) and letting it text you only for approvals.

**Skip it if** you are outside a supported country, you need EU availability, or you want a documented enterprise admin model. Muse is consumer-first today.

```text
Starter prompt to test Muse's judgment:
"Every Sunday, check my calendar for next week, find the two most
overbooked days, and propose moves. Do not send anything. Show me the plan."
```

### ChatGPT Dots

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/chatgpt-dots.png" name="ChatGPT Dots" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Dots are OpenAI's always-on agents inside ChatGPT. OpenAI's launch post (September 29, 2026) calls them agents that have their own cloud computer, learn from feedback and work toward goals around the clock, connecting through plugins to "over 4,000 apps". You can message or call a dot from ChatGPT on web, mobile and desktop, and message it in Slack and Teams. A texting beta over iMessage and RCS rolled out to Pro users in the US on October 28, 2026.

{% figure src="https://images.ctfassets.net/kftzwdyauwt9/3SQQ9jqSm3aytzUPKuNS3X/e30d9ff756987b178e2b141de5c64a35/Art_Card___Landscape___16_9___1920_1080.png?w=1600&h=900&fit=fill" alt="OpenAI's Introducing dots announcement card" caption="OpenAI's art card for the dots launch. Source: OpenAI, openai.com/index/introducing-dots" /%}

**Who it is for.** Knowledge workers and developers who want an agent to own a project (a deck, an app change, a content series) across days, with Codex and ChatGPT Work as the engines for heavy tasks. OpenAI's own examples include a dot noticing a forgotten invoice, preparing it and sending it after approval.

**Key features.** Own cloud computer you can open and inspect, optional connection to your laptop, Activity View, Custom Rules (allow, require approval, block), auto-review of consequential actions, "proactive research" that uses read-only access to connected apps, saved passwords used without exposing them to the model, voice calls, and and a mobile dot tab added on November 19, 2026 (not in the EEA, UK, Switzerland or Korea).

**Pros**

- Strongest documented controls: Custom Rules plus auto-review plus an activity log.
- Deep tool reach through the plugin ecosystem, plus Codex for work that needs code.
- Works in Slack and Teams, which matters if your team wants a shared surface.

**Cons**

- Availability is narrow. The help center says dots are for Pro and selected Plus users, outside the EEA, Switzerland and the UK, and that a downgrade from Pro to Plus loses your dot.
- The cheapest guaranteed route is Pro at $100 per month.
- OpenAI says tasks a dot starts in Codex or ChatGPT Work count toward normal limits, so heavy use is not free of metering.

**Benefits for you.** If your problem is ongoing project ownership rather than one-off errands, dots have the clearest delegation model: you set rules, it works, you review. The Plus test is the thing to watch.

**Pricing (OpenAI Help Center, as of November 25, 2026).** First dot included in ChatGPT Pro and Business Premium at no extra cost. Pro plans are $100 (Pro 100), $200 (Pro 200) and $500 (Pro 500) per month, per OpenAI's Pro tiers article. OpenAI says conversations with a dot do not count toward ChatGPT usage limits, and says that in the future you will be able to add more dots and scale each one. Enterprise dots have no fee through January 13, 2027, with billing starting January 14, 2027.

**Free tier and limits.** No free tier. On November 19, 2026 OpenAI's Thibault Sottiaux wrote on X that OpenAI is testing dots with a small number of ChatGPT Plus users and bringing them to all ChatGPT Business Standard users, and the help center says Plus is "gradually rolling out" to select accounts. Per press coverage of that post, the Business Standard rollout covers eligible US members, with one personal dot each and unlisted usage limits. Support cannot enable a dot for you.

{% x id="2123556256392847657" /%}

{% callout type="info" %}
OpenAI's pricing page lists "Dot, your always-on agent" under Pro only as of November 25, 2026, while its help center says select Plus users are getting access. Treat Plus as a limited test, not a purchasing reason.
{% /callout %}

**Standout use case.** A multi-day project with deliverables (analysis, deck, app change) that you want to supervise by exception.

**Skip it if** you are in the EU, UK or Switzerland as an individual, or you will not pay $100 per month for the first dot.

For a deeper look at its permission model, see our post on [OpenAI dots permissions and workflows](https://agentskills.codes/blog/openai-dots-permissions-and-workflows).

### Claude Cowork

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/claude-cowork.png" name="Claude Cowork" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Cowork is Anthropic's agent for knowledge work. You give it a goal and it works across your files and connected tools, then hands back finished output such as a deck, spreadsheet or report. Anthropic's product page describes it as "give it a goal" work, and says you can start a task from your phone and let it keep working in the cloud.

{% figure src="https://assets.claude.com/9716b96b8df867765a639f3ea8a07170508200bf.jpg?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="Claude Cowork product image" caption="Claude Cowork product image. Source: Anthropic, claude.com/product/cowork" /%}

**How it works.** Anthropic's support article says that since October 6, 2026, new Cowork tasks on Pro, Max and Ultra plans run in the cloud. You can close your laptop, and scheduled tasks run without any of your devices being online. The older "Only on your computer" option was removed. Desktop apps exist for macOS, Windows and Linux (Linux in beta), and web and mobile access works for Pro, Max, Ultra, Team (beta) and Enterprise when an owner enables it. On Team, cloud sessions are on by default. On Enterprise they are off by default and an admin has to switch them on.

**Who it is for.** Professionals who already pay for Claude and want a delegate for document-heavy work: research summaries, spreadsheets, slide drafts, recurring reports.

**Key features.** Goal-based tasks, file and folder access on desktop, connectors, scheduled and handed-off tasks, a Claude in Chrome side panel on paid plans, and shared usage with the rest of your Claude plan.

**Pros**

- No extra product fee: it is included in every paid Claude plan.
- Cloud execution means scheduled work does not depend on a laptop being awake.
- Strong admin controls on Team and Enterprise.

**Cons**

- It is a work agent, not a life-admin agent. It does not text you, call businesses or shop for you.
- Usage is shared with the rest of your Claude plan, so heavy agent use can drain chat limits.
- There is no free tier.

**Benefits for you.** If you already use Claude every day, Cowork is the lowest-friction way to get agent behaviour with no new account, no new bill and a familiar permission model.

**Pricing (claude.com/pricing, as of November 25, 2026).** Free $0 (no hand-off or scheduled tasks). Pro $17 per month billed annually or $20 billed monthly. Max $100 per month (5x Pro usage). Ultra $200 per month (10x Pro weekly usage). Team Standard $25 monthly or $20 annual per seat; Team Premium $125 monthly or $100 annual per seat, per Anthropic's support pages. "Hand off and schedule tasks" requires Pro or above.

**Free tier and limits.** None for Cowork. Tasks count against the plan's 5-hour and weekly limits.

**Standout use case.** A Monday-morning scheduled task that pulls last week's numbers into a draft report while your laptop stays closed.

**Skip it if** you want an agent that handles shopping, bookings or phone calls, or you need a free option.

```text
Starter task for Cowork:
"Every Friday at 4pm, read the files in /Reports, summarise what changed
this week in five bullets, and save a draft to /Reports/weekly.md.
Ask me before overwriting anything."
```

### Claude Duet

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/claude-duet.png" name="Claude Duet" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Duet is a separate Anthropic app where Claude works as a proactive agent. Anthropic's announcement on October 21, 2026 said it "comes to you with what needs your attention, completes tasks you ask of it, keeps working when you step away, and checks with you before it acts on your behalf."

{% x id="2112984664256241837" /%}

{% figure src="https://claude.com/static/claude-duet/og.jpg" alt="Claude Duet product image" caption="Claude Duet product image. Source: Anthropic, claude.com/product/claude-duet" /%}

**How it works.** Per Anthropic's help center, Duet is a research preview for Pro, Max and Ultra subscribers in the US, available by waitlist or invite, and it is not offered on Team or Enterprise plans. It runs Claude Opus 5.5 and you cannot pick a different model. It checks your connected accounts at set intervals, sends emails only with your approval, and has no phone calls or texting. Anthropic lists trading and moving money as off-limits and says Personal Finance is unavailable. There are iOS and Android apps plus claude.ai.

**Who it is for.** Professionals and small-business owners. Anthropic's own article says it is built for them, and that personal errands such as booking tickets are weaker today.

**Key features.** Proactive surfacing of what needs attention, background work, approval before actions, connected accounts, and mobile apps.

**Pros**

- Included in an existing Pro, Max or Ultra plan at no extra cost.
- Clear hard limits (no money movement, approval before emails) make its behaviour predictable.
- Anthropic documents what it cannot do, which is rarer than it should be.

**Cons**

- US only, waitlist, and a research preview that can change.
- Its usage shares the same 5-hour and weekly limits as the rest of your Claude plan, and background work counts.
- Anthropic says you cannot watch its browser while it works.

**Benefits for you.** If your pain is an overflowing inbox and calendar rather than ongoing projects, Duet is aimed at exactly that. You do not pay a separate fee to try it once you have access.

**Pricing (Anthropic help center, as of November 25, 2026).** Included in Pro, Max and Ultra at no extra cost; plan prices as listed under Cowork above. Anthropic's help article also describes one-time limit resets for early users (Pro 3, Max and Ultra 1) that expire December 15, 2026.

**Free tier and limits.** None. Free plan users are not eligible. Shared usage limits apply.

**Standout use case.** Letting it surface the threads that need a decision, with drafts waiting for your approval.

**Skip it if** you are outside the US, on a Team or Enterprise plan, or you want it to handle consumer errands.

### Gemini Agent (Tasks in Gemini)

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/gemini-agent.png" name="Gemini Agent" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's agent started as Gemini Agent in November 2025, became Gemini Spark (a 24/7 agent for AI Ultra) in May 2026, and on November 17, 2026 was renamed Tasks in Gemini and made free. The Verge reported that it is powered by Gemini 4 Hydrogen and now reaches all users in the US, Canada, Japan, Korea, Australia and India. You switch from "Chat" to "Tasks" on the Gemini home page.

**How it works.** Google's blog post on the launch says Tasks runs in a hardware-isolated virtual machine, asks permission before buying, and connects to Google apps plus more than 50 third-party apps such as OpenTable and Instacart. US users get extra abilities: calling businesses to compare prices or check stock, paying with Google Pay under a hard spend cap, and assigning tasks by texting Gemini or emailing gemini@google.com. Calling is not available in some regions and US states, and it needs a US phone number.

**Who it is for.** Anyone who wants to try an agent without paying, especially people already deep in Gmail, Calendar and Google Pay.

**Key features.** Tasks, Skills, Schedules and Connectors; texting and email intake; phone calls to businesses; payments with a cap; Mac computer use for Plus, Pro and Ultra subscribers on Apple silicon (announced November 19, 2026, US English).

**Pros**

- Among the free options here, the one that documents calling and payments (US only).
- The spend cap and ask-before-buying rule are a sensible default for a first agent.
- Email and text intake means you do not need a new app habit.

**Cons**

- The most advanced features are US-only, and some countries get only the basic version.
- It is tied to the Google ecosystem. If you live in Microsoft 365, the connectors are a weaker fit.
- Google's naming has changed three times in a year, so documentation and tutorials are inconsistent.

**Benefits for you.** The cheapest path from zero to a working agent. You can try it on a real task today with a free Google account.

**Pricing (Google, as of November 25, 2026).** Tasks in Gemini: free in the six countries above, per gemini.google. Google AI Plus and Pro add higher usage limits; Engadget reported AI Pro at $20 per month in July 2026 (Google's subscription page we fetched did not show the Pro price). Google AI Ultra is $99.99 per month (5x Pro usage limits) or $199.99 per month (20x), per gemini.google/subscriptions.

**Free tier and limits.** Free, with usage limits that Google does not publish as a number.

**Standout use case.** "Find three dentists near me with Saturday availability, call them, and text me the options." (Calling is US only.)

**Skip it if** you are outside the six launch countries or you need enterprise controls today.

### Kimi Work

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/kimi-work.png" name="Kimi Work" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Kimi Work is a local agent in the Work mode of Moonshot's Kimi desktop app. Its help center says it runs on Mac with Apple silicon (macOS 12 or later) and Windows 10 or later. It launched June 3, 2026 and is labelled Beta. Per the help center, newer versions are powered by Kimi K3, though June press coverage named K2.6, so the model name has changed since launch.

**How it works.** Because it runs on your computer, it can read and write local files and drive your local browser through a browser extension. It supports Agent Swarm with up to 300 sub-agents, scheduled tasks, skills, plugins and three permission levels (default, manual approval and fully automatic). A Decrypt report in June 2026 noted that tasks stop when the laptop closes, and that Moonshot's separate Kimi Claw product is the 24/7 cloud version.

**Who it is for.** People who want an agent working on their own files and are comfortable leaving a laptop on, including researchers and analysts who value parallel sub-agents.

**Key features.** Local file access, Agent Swarm, scheduling, browser extension, widgets and a dashboard, and skill support.

**Pros**

- Your files stay local unless you move them.
- Agent Swarm is a distinctive feature for large research and extraction jobs.
- Three clear permission levels.

**Cons**

- Beta, and it stops when your laptop sleeps or closes.
- A Chinese vendor may not fit some corporate or regulated data policies. Check your own policy before connecting sensitive accounts.
- Credits are shared across features, so heavy swarms can burn through a plan quickly.

**Benefits for you.** Best when the job is "work on my files", not "run my life". It complements a cloud agent rather than replacing it.

**Pricing (Kimi Help Center pricing article, captured October 19, 2026).** Plus $19 per month ($180 per year), Pro $39 ($372), Max $99 ($948), Ultra $199 ($1,908), all in USD, on a shared monthly credit pool that does not roll over. The older Moderato, Allegretto, Allegro and Vivace names are now legacy plans. Decrypt reported in June 2026 that the download is free.

**Free tier and limits.** Free to download; the help center says to check the membership page for what free accounts can do, and we did not confirm a free agent allowance.

**Standout use case.** Fan out 50 sub-agents to summarise a folder of 500 PDFs overnight, then review the combined report in the morning.

**Skip it if** you need a cloud agent that runs while your laptop is shut, or your company bans the vendor.

### Manus

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/manus.png" name="Manus" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Manus is a general-purpose autonomous agent that works in its own cloud sandbox: research, slides, websites and multi-step web tasks. It also has scheduled automations.

{% figure src="https://files.manuscdn.com/webapp/media/ogBanner.png" alt="Manus homepage banner" caption="Manus banner image. Source: Manus, manus.im" /%}

**Ownership note.** Meta announced it would acquire Manus in December 2025. Press reports (Reuters and CNBC, via secondary summaries) said China's NDRC ordered the deal withdrawn in April 2026, and Manus said it resumed independent operations on September 1, 2026. We only read these through third-party summaries, so treat the ownership history as reported, not confirmed by us. It matters for this post because Meta now sells its own agent, Muse.

**Who it is for.** Individuals who want a flexible "do it for me" agent for research and deliverables, without tying it to a single ecosystem.

**Key features.** Cloud sandbox, wide research, slide and site generation, concurrent tasks, scheduled automations, and a credit system.

**Pros**

- A real free plan with daily credits.
- Strong at research and presentable output.
- It is not tied to one model vendor or ecosystem.

**Cons**

- Credit-based pricing makes monthly cost hard to predict.
- Its own pages disagree on plans (see below).
- We found no documented WhatsApp or text-message access like Muse and Gemini offer.

**Benefits for you.** A place to test an agent before committing to an ecosystem.

**Pricing (conflicting, as of November 25, 2026).** Manus's help center article on membership pricing lists Free ($0, 300 daily credits, 1 concurrent task, 2 scheduled automations), Pro from $20 per month (4,000 credits; 17% off annual) and a $40 tier (8,000 credits), and Team from $20 per seat. A third-party tracker (aidia.pengi-n.co.jp) that read manus.im/pricing on November 25, 2026 reported different figures: Pro $100 for 20,000 credits and Max $200 for 40,000, plus a time-limited Black Friday discount. The pricing page did not render for our fetch, so we cannot say which is current. Check manus.im/pricing before you buy.

**Free tier and limits.** Daily credits on the free plan, usable only with the lightest model, and Manus's help center says free users are capped at 1,500 daily-refresh credits a month.

**Standout use case.** Turning a messy research question into a sourced slide deck.

**Skip it if** you want predictable monthly billing, or you want an agent that lives in your messages.

### Genspark

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/genspark.png" name="Genspark" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Genspark's Super Agent is a project workspace that runs on its own cloud computer. Its help center (updated November 16, 2026) says it can run up to two hours in one turn and keeps going after you close the tab.

**How it works.** You describe a project, it plans and executes in its cloud environment, and you steer it with follow-up messages. It supports Skills, and offers Standard and Ultra modes that spend different amounts of credits per message (10 to 60 for Standard, 50 to 500 for Ultra, per Genspark).

**Who it is for.** People who produce slides, spreadsheets, docs and research on a regular basis and want one place to do it, rather than a life-admin agent.

**Key features.** Project cloud computer, long-running turns, file creation and export, Skills, and a classic mode.

**Pros**

- Free plan available, and monthly plans cost less than Pro-tier rivals.
- Long autonomous turns suit big deliverables.
- Credit pricing is published in detail.

**Cons**

- Credit costs vary widely by mode, so a heavy month is hard to forecast.
- The free plan cannot download or export files, according to Genspark's help center.
- We found no messaging-app or phone-call features documented.

**Benefits for you.** If your agent use is mostly deliverables, Genspark's price per output can be lower than the premium assistant plans.

**Pricing (Genspark help center, updated November 20, 2026).** Free: 100 credits per day and 1 GB of storage. Plus: $24.99 per month, or $19.99 per month billed annually ($239.99 a year), with 10,000 credits a month and 50 GB. Pro: $249.99 per month or $199.99 per month billed annually ($2,399.99), with 125,000 credits and 1 TB. Team: $40 per seat per month ($35 annual). Credit pack: $20 for 7,500 credits. Genspark lists some benefits as valid through June 30, 2027.

**Free tier and limits.** 100 credits a day; no file downloads or exports.

**Standout use case.** A long-running market-research project that ends in a slide deck and a spreadsheet.

**Skip it if** you need inbox, calendar or phone integration, or you need predictable per-seat pricing.

### Grok Bot

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/grok-bot.png" name="Grok Bot" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Grok Bot, launched by xAI on August 11, 2026 (the company appears as SpaceXSI on its pages), is a team of always-on agents with their own computer. Its launch post says Bots "sign into the tools you already use", including "platforms with no clean API or MCP", and keep working 24/7.

{% figure src="https://x.ai/images/news/introducing-grok-bot-og-2.png" alt="Introducing Grok Bot announcement image" caption="Grok Bot announcement image. Source: xAI, x.ai/news/introducing-grok-bot" /%}

**How it works.** The Grok Bot FAQ (last updated November 13, 2026) says every Bot on your account shares one persistent cloud computer with shared files, browser sessions and logins, and warns against using separate Bots as a security boundary. Bot work continues when your laptop is closed. A Bot only runs commands on your own desktop if you allow it, and by default it asks each time. You can reach it from desktop apps on macOS, Windows and Linux, iOS and Android, X Chat, Slack, Microsoft Teams, or by tagging @bot on X.

**Who it is for.** Teams and power users who want multiple specialist Bots (inbox, CRM, expenses, bug triage) under one account.

**Key features.** Multiple Bots on one computer, approvals, a vault for sign-ins, skills and routines, Team Bots for Slack and Teams, and meeting attendance.

**Pros**

- Works in apps with no API, which is the main gap for pure-API agents.
- Usage is separate from your Grok chat plan, per the launch post.
- Wide surface area: X, Slack, Teams, mobile and desktop.

**Cons**

- Beta, and the access rules have changed since launch. The August launch post named SuperGrok and Cursor plans, while the November FAQ names The X Subscription Premium, Super and Ultra.
- Bots share one computer, so there is no isolation between them.
- Signing in uses a Cursor account, which surprises users who expect a Grok login.

**Benefits for you.** The best option here if you want several named agents, each owning a lane, instead of one assistant.

**Pricing (xAI documentation, as of November 25, 2026).** Included in The X Subscription Premium, Super and Ultra, not in Free or Plus, with a shared weekly usage pool. xAI's subscription pricing page (October 11, 2026) lists Free $0, Plus $8, Premium $30 ($300 a year), Super $100 ($1,000 a year) and Ultra $200 ($2,000 a year). We read these prices from xAI's docs; the pricing page itself did not render cleanly for us.

**Free tier and limits.** No free access. Some accounts see a short trial usage credit.

**Standout use case.** A "chief of staff" Bot that delegates to an inbox Bot and an expenses Bot.

**Skip it if** you want a single polished assistant, or you do not want your agent tied to a Cursor sign-in.

### Perplexity Comet

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-personal-ai-agents-2026/logos/perplexity-comet.png" name="Perplexity Comet" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Comet is Perplexity's browser. The browser itself is free on Mac, Windows, iOS and Android, and it includes the Comet Assistant that can browse and act for you. Perplexity's help center lists Browser Agent queries as unavailable on Free, with monthly limits on Pro and higher limits on Max.

**How it works.** The assistant works in your own browser session, so it can use sites you are already logged into. According to a secondary source (itsalive), its Work mode hands bigger jobs to Perplexity Computer, a paid, credit-based product. Perplexity's changelog entries for November 6 and 24, 2026 mention a fully local Computer on Mac and multi-agent work in Computer. We could not load Perplexity's changelog page directly (it returned 403), so treat those details as secondary.

**Who it is for.** Researchers and heavy web users who want an agent in the browser they already use, rather than a separate VM.

**Key features.** Browser-native assistant, research with citations, local or cloud task hand-off, and mobile browsers.

**Pros**

- It acts inside your real logged-in browser session.
- Free to install, so you can judge the interface at no cost.
- Tight integration with Perplexity's research strengths.

**Cons**

- The acting agent is metered and not on the free plan.
- It is less "always-on" than VM-based agents: we found no documentation of texting you or running proactive background work in the browser agent.
- Letting any browser agent act in logged-in sessions raises prompt-injection risk, so keep sensitive accounts out of scope.

**Benefits for you.** A low-commitment way to get agent-style help while you browse, especially for research-heavy work.

**Pricing (Perplexity pricing page and help center, as of November 25, 2026).** Free $0. Pro $20 per month ($17 per month billed annually). Max $200 per month ($167 per month billed annually). Browser Agent query limits differ by plan and are not published as a single number.

**Free tier and limits.** The browser is free; the Browser Agent is not included on the Free plan.

**Standout use case.** Comparing five products across vendor sites while you are logged in, then summarising the differences.

**Skip it if** you want a persistent agent that works while your browser is closed.

