## Head-to-head: the closest pairs

### Meta Muse vs ChatGPT Dots: which to pick

This is the pair people search ("chatgpt dots vs muse"). They share the same architecture: an always-on agent with its own cloud computer, a browser, connectors, and approval for sensitive actions. They differ in who they are built for and what they cost.

- **Choose Muse** if you want a free start, WhatsApp access and phone calls, and your tasks are personal: reservations, shopping, calendar and fitness.
- **Choose Dots** if your tasks are projects with deliverables, you work in Slack or Teams, and you will pay $100 a month for the first dot.
- **Safety models differ.** Meta describes a Sentinel that checks every outside action and a credential vault. OpenAI describes Custom Rules, auto-review and an Activity View. Neither has been independently audited in public, as far as we found.
- **Availability differs.** Muse is in limited testing in a handful of countries. Dots exclude the EEA, UK and Switzerland.

### Claude Cowork vs Claude Duet: which to pick

They are two products from one company. Cowork is task-oriented: you give it a goal and it produces files. Duet is proactive: it surfaces what needs attention.

- **Choose Cowork** if you have a clear job (a report, a deck) and want it available now on any paid plan.
- **Choose Duet** if your problem is triage, and you can get past the US waitlist.
- Both share your plan's usage limits, so running both heavily on Pro can be tight.

### Gemini Agent vs Muse: which free agent to pick

- **Choose Gemini** if you live in Google apps, want calling and Google Pay with a spend cap, and are in one of the six launch countries.
- **Choose Muse** if you prefer WhatsApp and a persistent VM you can open and watch, and you are in a supported market.
- Both ask before buying. Gemini publishes a hard spend cap. Meta's pages we read describe approval but did not mention a cap.

### Meta Muse vs Claude Duet: which to pick

- **Choose Muse** for consumer errands and phone calls. Meta's pages describe shopping, reservations and fitness plans.
- **Choose Duet** for professional triage. Anthropic says it is built for professionals and small-business owners, and that personal errands are weaker.
- **Cost.** Muse has a free tier. Duet needs a Pro plan at $20 a month, plus an invite.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

Hosted personal agents like the ones above are closed products, so you cannot install a skill into Muse or Dots the way you would into Claude Code or Codex. Skills and MCP servers matter in two cases: when you run your own agent alongside a hosted one (for example Claude Code, Codex or Hermes on a server), and when you want to prepare the same Gmail, calendar and research workflows in a form you control. These are real entries in the agentskills.codes catalog, each confirmed to load (HTTP 200) on November 25, 2026. The one-line reasons come from each skill's catalog description, and we did not install or run them for this post.

| Skill | Why it fits a personal-agent workflow |
| --- | --- |
| [gog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/gog) | A Google Workspace CLI for Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Contacts, Sheets and Docs. Useful for scripting the same inbox and calendar jobs you would hand to Gemini or Muse. |
| [gmail-manager](https://agentskills.codes/skills/gmail-manager) | Send, read and search Gmail, and manage labels and drafts. A local equivalent of the inbox triage most of these agents advertise. |
| [google-calendar-sync](https://agentskills.codes/skills/google-calendar-sync) | Create, read, update and delete Google Calendar events through the Calendar API, for the scheduling jobs people give Duet or Muse. |
| [email-action-extractor](https://agentskills.codes/skills/email-action-extractor) | Pulls actionable tasks out of email text and filters the informational noise, which is the core of a daily brief. |
| [browser-automation](https://agentskills.codes/skills/browser-automation) | Drives a browser through natural-language CLI commands, the same class of capability as Comet or a VM browser. |
| [playwright-browser-automation](https://agentskills.codes/skills/playwright-browser-automation) | A more deterministic browser option built on Playwright when you want repeatable steps instead of an open-ended agent. |
| [connect-apps](https://agentskills.codes/skills/connect-apps) | Connects Claude to apps like Gmail, Slack and GitHub, so a self-run agent can reach the same tools hosted agents use through plugins. |
| [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) | A guide for building MCP servers, for when the app your agent needs has no connector. |
| [notion-meeting-intelligence](https://agentskills.codes/skills/notion-meeting-intelligence) | Prepares meeting materials from Notion context, a common "chief of staff" task. |
| [research-lookup](https://agentskills.codes/skills/research-lookup) | Looks up current research information using Perplexity's Sonar models, for agents that need a citation-style search step. |

A note on security: giving any agent a skill that can send email or drive a browser adds the same risks the hosted vendors worry about. Prompt injection through email or web pages is the main one. Meta's safety post says its agent is built to assume it may be under attack, and the same assumption is sensible for a DIY setup: limit scopes, require approval for sends, and keep secrets out of the agent's context.

Related reading on this site: [OpenAI dots permissions and workflows](https://agentskills.codes/blog/openai-dots-permissions-and-workflows), [Muse Wonder and Meta's scorecard for agent builders](https://agentskills.codes/blog/muse-wonder-meta-scorecard-agent-builders), [Claude Code Remote Control for skill workflows](https://agentskills.codes/blog/claude-code-remote-control-for-skill-workflows) and [Kimi K3.1 Pro for agent skills](https://agentskills.codes/blog/kimi-k3-1-pro-for-agent-skills).

