## Common mistakes when choosing a personal agent

1. **Treating a waitlist as availability.** Duet is a US waitlist, Muse is in limited testing, and Dots on Plus is a test. Check that you can actually get in before you plan around one.
2. **Comparing headline prices without the unit.** Muse counts tokens and phone calls, Genspark and Manus count credits, Claude counts shared usage windows. A $20 plan on one is not equal to a $20 plan on another.
3. **Giving full inbox access on day one.** Start with read-only or approval-required scopes. OpenAI's proactive research is read-only by design, and that is a reasonable template.
4. **Assuming the agent keeps running.** Kimi Work stops when the laptop closes. Comet is a browser agent. Cloud VM agents (Muse, Dots, Cowork, Duet, Gemini, Grok Bot, Genspark, Manus) keep working.
5. **Ignoring regional limits.** Dots exclude the EEA, UK and Switzerland. Gemini's calling is US only. Kimi's and Manus's regional terms are worth reading too.
6. **Treating one vendor's blog as a benchmark.** No neutral benchmark exists for personal agents. Test on your own three jobs.
7. **Letting Bots share a computer and calling it isolation.** xAI's own FAQ says not to use separate Bots as a security boundary.

## Buying and setup checklist

1. Write down your three real jobs (for example: weekly calendar clean-up, inbox triage, one monthly report).
2. Check availability for your country and plan before you pay anything.
3. Start on a free tier where one exists (Gemini, Muse, Manus, Genspark) and run the same three jobs on two products.
4. Connect one account at a time, with approvals on for sends and purchases.
5. Review the audit trail, Activity View or task log after each run.
6. Set a spend cap or monthly budget before you enable payments (Gemini offers one).
7. Re-check pricing on the vendor's page before upgrading. Several products here changed plans during the past two months.

## How we evaluated

This is a documentation-and-sources review, not a hands-on test. On November 25, 2026 we fetched the vendors' own pricing, help-center and launch pages where they loaded, searched for press coverage to fill gaps, and checked every link, video and post we cite. We did not subscribe to any product, create an account, connect an inbox or run any agent task, and we make no claim about how well any of them performs on a real job.

What we did run: HTTP checks on every URL used (200 unless noted), YouTube oEmbed lookups for the embedded video, X syndication lookups for the embedded posts (confirming author and text), header checks on each image, and catalog page checks for each skill link. Details are in the verification ledger that accompanies this post.

Known gaps. Meta's launch newsroom page returned almost no text to our fetch, so we relied on Meta's help center, muse.ai and security.muse.ai. Manus's pricing page did not render for our fetch, and its own help center disagrees with a third-party reading of the live page, so we report both. Perplexity's changelog returned 403. We could not confirm Kimi Work's free-tier allowance. Gemini AI Pro's price came from Engadget in July 2026, not from Google's page.

We rank nothing by quality. The order of the product sections follows search demand, and the picks are labelled as our reading of documented features. No product paid for inclusion, and none of the links are affiliate links.

## FAQ

### What is Meta's AI agent?

Meta's AI agent is Muse, which launched on September 8, 2026. Meta describes it as a personal agent that sorts email, books reservations, shops, manages calendars and builds fitness plans. It runs on its own secure cloud computer, asks before sensitive actions such as sending an email or making a purchase, and can be used in a Muse app or in WhatsApp.

### Is Muse AI free?

Yes, with a limit. Meta's help center lists a free plan with a usage limit that it does not state in numbers. Paid plans are Power at $20 a month (500M Muse tokens a week and 10 phone calls a day) and Maximum at $100 a month (3B tokens a week and 20 calls a day), as of November 25, 2026. Meta also says Muse is in limited testing and not available in all locations yet.

### What is the difference between Meta Muse and ChatGPT Dots?

Both are always-on agents with their own cloud computer, but they are built for different users. Muse is consumer-first, has a free tier, supports WhatsApp and adds phone calls on paid plans. Dots are positioned for ongoing work projects, connect to more than 4,000 apps through plugins, work in Slack and Teams, and start at the $100 Pro plan, with only a small Plus test so far. Dots exclude the EEA, UK and Switzerland.

### Is ChatGPT Dots free?

No. As of November 25, 2026, OpenAI includes your first dot in ChatGPT Pro and Business Premium, and enterprise plans can enable it. OpenAI says Plus access is being tested with select users. OpenAI's pricing page lists the dot under Pro only. The three Pro plans are $100, $200 and $500 a month.

### What is the difference between Claude Cowork and Claude Duet?

Cowork is a goal-based agent for files and tools: you assign a task and get a finished document back, and it runs in the cloud since October 6, 2026. Duet is a proactive app that surfaces what needs your attention and works in the background. Duet is a US-only research preview with a waitlist, available to Pro, Max and Ultra but not Team or Enterprise. Cowork is available on all paid plans.

### Which personal AI agent is best for beginners?

Gemini's Tasks is the easiest to try because it is free in the US, Canada, Australia, India, Japan and Korea, and asks permission before buying. Muse is the second easiest if you are in a supported location and want WhatsApp access. Paid-only options like Cowork or Dots make more sense once you know what job you want to delegate.

### Which personal AI agent can make phone calls?

Muse (paid plans, with 10 or 20 calls a day) and Gemini's Tasks (calling businesses, US only, not available in some regions) document phone calling. Anthropic says Duet has no phone calls or texts. We did not find phone calling documented for the others.

### Are personal AI agents safe to connect to my email?

They reduce some risks and add others. Vendors document approval gates, isolated virtual machines and credential handling (Meta's Sentinel, OpenAI's Custom Rules, Gemini's spend cap). But all of these agents can still make mistakes, and reading email or web pages creates prompt-injection risk. Start with read-only scopes, require approval for sending and paying, and review the activity log.

### Can I use these agents in the EU or UK?

It depends on the product. OpenAI's help center says dots are for users outside the EEA, Switzerland and the UK. Google lists six countries for free Tasks. Meta says Muse is not available in all locations. Anthropic says Duet is US only. Cowork's availability follows your Claude plan. Check each vendor's availability page for your country.

### Are Kimi Work and Perplexity Comet real agents or just assistants?

Both can take multi-step actions. Kimi Work runs locally on your computer and can use Agent Swarm sub-agents, but it stops when the laptop closes. Comet acts inside your browser session, but its acting agent is a paid feature with monthly limits. Neither is an always-on cloud agent in the way Muse, Dots or Grok Bot are.

### How much does a personal AI agent cost per month?

From $0 to $200 or more. Gemini Tasks and Muse's base tier are free, Cowork and Duet use your $20 Claude Pro plan, Genspark Plus is $24.99, Kimi Plus is $19, Grok Bot needs a $30 Premium plan, and Dots start at $100. Manus's pricing is unclear between sources. All prices are as of November 25, 2026 and change often.

### Do I need an agent that runs in the cloud?

If you want it to work while your laptop is closed, yes. Muse, Dots, Cowork, Duet, Gemini, Grok Bot, Genspark and Manus run in the cloud. Kimi Work is local and Comet is browser-based. A local agent is better when you want files to stay on your machine.

### Can I use these with Claude Code or Hermes skills?

The hosted agents have their own plugin and skill systems (Gemini, Kimi, Grok Bot and Genspark all document skills). Our catalog skills run in agents like Claude Code, Codex and Hermes. See the skills section above for Gmail, Calendar, browser and MCP entries that you can run yourself.

